What have I learnt?

This project taught me how different anonymisation techniques work in practice and how they affect the privacy risk of a dataset. 
I learned the differences between hashing, tokenisation, redaction, and encryption, and how each method protects data in a different way. 
Building the tool helped me understand how organisations prepare sensitive information before using it for testing or analysis.
I also learned how important logging and audit trails are when handling personal data. 
Adding logs made the anonymisation process transparent and helped me verify that each field was processed correctly. 
Creating a configurable system through config.json taught me how to separate logic from settings, making the tool easier to maintain and extend.

Set-backs

I faced several setbacks while building this project. At first, I tried to encrypt integers directly, which caused errors because encryption requires byte strings. 
Fixing this taught me how to properly handle data types when using cryptography. 
I also had issues with inconsistent CSV formatting, spacing errors, and missing fields, which helped me understand how messy real-world data can be. 
Another challenge was managing the encryption key — I had to learn how to generate, store, and reuse a stable key so encrypted values remained consistent across runs.

Conclusion
Overall, this project strengthened my understanding of secure data handling, improved my Python skills, and gave me practical experience with privacy engineering concepts.
